first I'm interested to get your  reaction to the resignation of amber art  she mentioned the expulsion of the 21  and peace including yourself as part of  the reason for resigning not only from  tablet but also the concept of when well  I think for me the principal issue is  that she's been clear that there isn't  really a negotiation going on with the  EU to try and get any kind of brexit  deal and from my perspective I've been  waiting to see the Prime Minister get on  a plane to Brussels that's not happened  yet and so my concerns always been that  there isn't really a negotiation  happening on brexit now and therefore  the main objective of the government is  to leave with no deal and I think  certainly for my community that's been  completely unacceptable and that's why I  voted for this bill this week which  simply allows Parliament a binding vote  on any deal were borås to get one but  also a binding vote on no deal and  that's how we run Britain it's a  parliamentary democracy and I think it's  incredibly important to protect that so  I welcome that amber has even after we  had the vote chosen to support it at  same time you talked to you there about  trying to stop no deal you could have  done that by voting for a deal under to  resume  well it was clear to me that that deal  was never going to get through  Parliament I said back in the people  well I said in July last year actually  that it was obvious it didn't deliver on  the referendum as far as leavers was  concerned and for communities like mine  which are overwhelmingly remained people  just thought what's the point we're  following all the rules it's far smarter  to still be sat around the table setting  them so I think in reality it was clear  that was a brick wall the government  chose to run into it three times in  spite of it being obvious to me that it  wasn't a viable strategy okay and in  response to amber on to them resignation  you can have a quick look at something  your colleague David Gork tweeted at a  former conservative like yourself he  said and the rod has been  extraordinarily brave but her concerns  about how the government is behaving  reflect the views of many of my former  colleagues one way or another it's time  for them to act I mean do you agree with  David gorg is it time for more  conservative MPs to make their  unhappiness known I believe many will  this weekend be trying to work out the  right thing to do I think the reality is  for me it's impossible to stand by and  simply allow a government to inflict and  no deal brexit on Britain for me it is  the most profoundly unconservative  policy I have ever seen it destroys jobs  it harms our economy it damages  opportunity it will damage social  mobility and I find it hard to reconcile  that with the Conservative Party that's  for me always been about a vehicle for  social mobility and one of the reasons I  said I couldn't run as a Conservative  candidate on Tuesday this week was  because I think the Conservative Party  is now fixated on brexit brexit is its  priority above everything else and that  is simply not a priority for me when I  compare it to the chance to work on  social mobility and equality of  opportunity I think that's the real  issue facing Britain and when I look at  communities like the one I grew up in  Rotherham I would much rather be working  on the ground trying to practically make  a difference and I think the change that  people in those communities need is  actually access to opportunity I think  brexit takes them further away from the  solutions that I believe they deserve I  mean you mentioned there growing up in  bothering me you were the first  education secretary to be educated at a  comprehensive school which is a shocking  shocking indictment on Britain actually  that was the case if you if you were you  know that young girl in Rotherham would  you still be attracted to the  Conservative Party today  I think I'd see it as a party that was  about brexit 